Here are a couple of theories to expand the toilet training idea.
While I don't deny the importance of specific traumas, I suspect
that looking at your (or other people's) parents' habits during
their upbringing can be useful. Is this case, what were the
parents' ratios between complaining, action, and enjoyment?

Also, in my own life, I've looked at why people repeat themselves.
(I've especially looked at why people, including me, repeat themselves
in conversation because I get bored. :-)  ) I've come to the general
conclusion that people repeat themselves because they're looking
for a response or emotional effect that they haven't been getting
and that becoming conscious of what they're looking for can help
in choosing a more useful behavior.

As for angst tape loops, I've found for myself that if I pay
attention to my emotions as I'm speaking, I don't feel any
need to say the same thing more than a few times. (The number
of times does go up if it's more serious, but there still
seems to be more progress per time, and I've got better at
letting go of things since I started the policy.)
