You've brought up an interesting question,I think, one that cuts right to
the heart of libertarian theory, and one I'm still trying to work out my
own personal answer for. The issue is that of "externalities." I think of
blindness and other "physically challenging" characteristics not the norm,
and therefore not accomodated by the basic marketplace, as being
statistical inevitabilities in any society, but especially one as big as
ours. If a blind person is limited as to his choices by his "handicap,"
then one way or another that limitation has to be factored into the
aggregate cost to society of supporting its members. Perhaps the cost
falls mostly to the blind person. I would propose that his blindness is an
externality, a cost borne by society but not in fact borne by any
individual in significant degree. There are means of accomodating blind
people which cost a lot to either the blind person or the state, neither
of which seems appropriate.

Rather, shouldn't the cost of accomodating people to the extent they need
be filtered somehow into the cost of the products we all buy? This would
be a true free market approach to internalizing externalities, it seems
to me. Don't take offence at my juxtaposition of examples, but the
question of disposing of packaging materials on the goods we buy is a
similar issue. We have a choice on garbage management -- leave it to the
government, which means people are almost always either overcharged or
undercharged; or build the cost of disposal into the price of owning a
product, probably through charging directly for garbage removal. I think
the latter is far saner and more fair.

Now I'd also postulate that the fact of non-blind people not being blind
is a benefit which should be paid for. If the incidence of blindness is
one in 10,000, then 1/10,000 of the cost of providing a blind person with
sufficient accomodation for a satisfactorily normal lifestyle should
accrue to each non-blind person. I don't have a proposal for how this
would be collected (not through taxes, though), but the fact that I'm not
blind and that I benefit from that fact and that there is this statistical
inevitability that one in 10,000 people (or whatever) will not have this
benefit implies to me that I'm getting something for free. And for me that
entirely counteracts the libertarian philosophy.

Any takers on this?





